from <math> import tanh;

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Saturable inductor and saturable transformer — smooth single-valued core
//
//  A core that saturates: the flux linkage keeps rising with current, but
//  past the knee only at the slope of the air path. One smooth curve, no
//  hysteresis:
//
//      lam(i) = Lsat i + (L0 - Lsat) i_k tanh(i / i_k),   i_k = lam_s / (L0 - Lsat)
//
//      lam ▲                    ___-----  slope Lsat
//          │               _.-''
//    lam_s ┼ - - - - - - .'  - - - - - - -  (the saturated asymptote,
//          │           /                     Lsat i + lam_s, meets i = 0 here)
//          │         /  slope L0
//          │       /
//          └──────┼───────────────────▶ i
//                i_k
//
//  The differential inductance dlam/di = Lsat + (L0 - Lsat) sech^2(i / i_k)
//  falls smoothly from L0 at zero current to Lsat in deep saturation.
//
//    L0     unsaturated inductance, H              N^2 mu0 mu_r Ae / le
//    Lsat   saturated (air-core) inductance, H     about N^2 mu0 Ae / le
//    lam_s  saturation flux linkage, Wb-turns      about N Bsat Ae
//
//  L0 > Lsat > 0 is required. Lsat = 0 would be a flux that can never exceed
//  lam_s, i.e. a winding that cannot carry the volt-seconds a source imposes
//  on it past saturation — no physical core does that, and the equations
//  would have no solution.
//
//  ── Why the current is the unknown ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  The winding is a branch, as in <magnetics> CoupledInductors: its current
//  is an MNA unknown and the branch voltage is R i + ddt(lam(i)). The curve
//  enters Newton through its slope dlam/di, which is bounded on both sides —
//  between Lsat and L0 — and never zero, so the branch is a companion
//  "resistance" (dlam/di)/h that stays positive and finite everywhere. There
//  is no exponential to overflow and no region of zero derivative, so no
//  limiter is needed: no nr_prev(), no nodiff(). tanh saturates to +-1 in
//  floating point without overflow, and its derivative underflows to exactly
//  0, leaving the Lsat slope — which is the physics.
//
//  The opposite choice, flux as the unknown with i = f(lam) through idt(),
//  puts 1/L(lam) in the Jacobian, which in .op (h -> 1e12 s) is a conductance
//  of order 1e12/Lsat; the branch form is an inductor at DC exactly as the L
//  primitive is, a short through R.
//
//  Measured on a sharp knee, L0 / Lsat = 1e5 (100 H / 1 mH, lam_s = 1.2 Wb,
//  1 Ohm, 325 V 50 Hz switched on at a zero crossing): ndf2 and bdf2 accept
//  every step in 2.1 Newton iterations on average, with 5-6 trust-region
//  rejections in the whole 100 ms run and no step-size collapse; the first
//  inrush peak, 232.92 A, is within 4.5e-5 of an independently integrated
//  reference (scipy Radau, rtol 1e-11).
//
//  ── Accuracy in saturation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  Deep in saturation a small error in flux is a large error in current
//  (di = dlam / Lsat). The flux is the time integral of the winding voltage,
//  so the Newton tolerance integrates into it, and it only decays with the
//  winding's L0/R — seconds, for a mains transformer. With the default
//  reltol (1e-3) a 100 ms inrush run of the SaturableTransformer below drifted
//  up to 2e-3 Wb at 5 us steps, which is a 1 % error in the saturated current
//  peaks; with `.solver(ndf2, 1e-6)` the drift stayed below 5e-7 Wb and the
//  inrush peak matched the reference to 1e-6. Tighten reltol for inrush
//  studies.
//
//  ── Using it ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  Without .op every core starts demagnetised (lam = 0): switching a
//  transformer on at a voltage zero crossing drives it to twice its normal
//  peak flux, which is the inrush case. There is no remanence, so the
//  worst case (remanent flux in the same direction) is not reproduced.
//  With .op the core starts at the DC operating point — the current a DC
//  voltage drives through the winding resistance.
//
//  Use ndf2 or bdf2: trapezoidal drifts on this model by 5 % of the inrush
//  peak within 100 ms, the runtime limitation described in coupled.hvr.
//
//  Not modelled: hysteresis, remanence, eddy-current and hysteresis loss,
//  temperature. Losses to first order: a resistor across the winding.
//
//  ── Verified ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  SaturableInductor L0 = 10 H, Lsat = 10 mH, lam_s = 1.2 Wb behind 2 Ohm,
//  325 V 50 Hz switched on at t = 0, against the reference ODE
//  L(i) di/dt = v - R i (scipy Radau, rtol 1e-11): inrush peak 55.6306 A vs
//  55.6351 A at a 50 us step ceiling (1.4e-4 of peak over the whole
//  waveform), 55.63498 A at 5 us (8.5e-6) — ndf2; bdf2 2.8e-4.
//  .op at a DC bias then .ac: the small-signal inductance is the curve's slope
//  at the bias to 1e-6 (at i = 0, i = i_k and i = 83 i_k).
//  Current-driven (5 A peak, 50 Hz, 40 x i_k), the terminal voltage is
//  (dlam/di) di/dt of the curve up to the integrator's differencing error,
//  which converges at second order: 2.7 % of the 15.7 kV peak at a 20 us step
//  ceiling, 2.3e-4 at 2 us.
//  SaturableTransformer (n = 0.06, Lm0 = 10 H, Lmsat = 10 mH, lam_s = 1.2 Wb,
//  Llp = 20 mH, Lls = 72 uH, Rp = 2 Ohm, Rs = 10 mOhm, 7.2 Ohm load) against
//  the two-current reference ODE: inrush peak 24.29350 A vs 24.29357 A.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Saturable inductor.
//   L0      unsaturated inductance, H (> Lsat)
//   Lsat    saturated inductance, H (> 0)
//   lam_s   saturation flux linkage, Wb-turns (> 0)
//   R       winding resistance, Ohm                         0
// Current: I(<instance>.br), positive from p to n.
analog module SaturableInductor<double L0, double Lsat, double lam_s, double R = 0>()
    [wire p, wire n]
{
    double i_k = lam_s / (L0 - Lsat);
    double i   = I(br);
    double lam = Lsat * i + (L0 - Lsat) * i_k * tanh(i / i_k);
    double v   = R * i + ddt(lam);
    voltage_source br<>(v) [p, n];
}

// Transformer with a saturable core: <magnetics> Transformer's T-model with
// the magnetising inductance replaced by the curve above, in the primary-
// referred magnetising current im = i1 + n i2.
//   n          turns ratio Ns/Np
//   Lm0        unsaturated magnetising inductance, primary side, H (> Lmsat)
//   Lmsat      saturated magnetising inductance, primary side, H (> 0)
//   lam_s      saturation flux linkage of the PRIMARY winding, Wb-turns
//   Llp, Lls   primary / secondary leakage inductance, H     0
//   Rp, Rs     primary / secondary winding resistance, Ohm   0
// Leakage flux does not saturate (it is in air).
analog module SaturableTransformer<double n, double Lm0, double Lmsat, double lam_s,
                                   double Llp = 0, double Lls = 0,
                                   double Rp = 0,  double Rs = 0>()
    [wire p1, wire n1, wire p2, wire n2]
{
    double i_k = lam_s / (Lm0 - Lmsat);
    double i1  = I(w1);
    double i2  = I(w2);
    double im  = i1 + n * i2;
    double lam = Lmsat * im + (Lm0 - Lmsat) * i_k * tanh(im / i_k);
    double v1  = Rp * i1 + ddt(Llp * i1 + lam);
    double v2  = Rs * i2 + ddt(Lls * i2 + n * lam);
    voltage_source w1<>(v1) [p1, n1];
    voltage_source w2<>(v2) [p2, n2];
}
